a ban on imports of russian coal comes  into effect in europe today eu countries  agreed on the embargo earlier this year  as part of sanctions over russia's  invasion of ukraine many european  countries have relied on russia to  supply energy in the form of coal much  of that coal was imported to europe  through the port of rotterdam in the  netherlands  let's cross over to rotterdam now where  our reporter barbara basil is standing  by  barbara the countries were rushing to  stock up on russian coal before the  embargo came into effect what's the  situation now  now we're in the harbor of rotterdam at  the cave where coal is unloaded you can  see one of those river barges that the  coal is put onto  right in the back here and further in  the back there are those big piles of  coal this is lignite so it doesn't  really look black there is a sort of  whitish residue on it but it this is the  stuff that really  goes into power plants that is mostly  used for electricity and the harbor of  rotterdam as of today declares no more  russian coal not a bucketful anywhere  here so they started fading it out there  there was a peak in in may about when  everyone was as you said stocking up on  coal trying to get in as much as  possible quickly but now it has gone  down to zero all the freighters that  used to be way out of sight of the  harbor  have been unloaded over the last two  three months and now it's all gone no  more will be coming in because european  countries say and of course the rob the  hub of rotterdam needs to enforce that  this is the end the embargo starts as of  today and they have to find coal in  different from different suppliers  now this uh embargo is coming into  effect barbara as europe is facing an  energy crisis could this embargo result  in a shortage of coal in europe this  winter leading to power cuts  yes indeed it's a possibility because  there are several factors that are  working together here that are  negative for the supply of  energy to europe  it's not only the coal they are trying  to replace the coal from south africa  even from ecuador from colombia that's  what the harbor told us so it all comes  through the sewers canal now instead of  from the northern russian harbors  but  even if they can sort of get the  quantities the problem also is the price  the price of the coal has tripled since  last year and the transport has become  incredibly difficult because the rhine  the river rhine which is the main  conduit to to transport the coal into  europe is drying up and so the river  barges like the one we see here in the  back it can can't really be loaded very  much anymore only a third of their cargo  and so a lot of barges have to go which  again increases the price and it might  be difficult for up to the winter to get  enough coal in to supply the power  plants and of course  the steel industry that uses coal  now not all eu countries are exposed to  this uh to the to this embargo quite as  much in terms of their loss of coal  supplies which countries are most going  to be most affected by this uh coal  embargo barbara  yeah again it was germany as was the gas  germany imported  14 million tons of russian coal  a year even a year ago and about half of  their imports came from russia so they  were extremely reliant now they have of  course started since the beginning of  the war they have started to replace  this coal from on the sides but as i  said it's a question of the price it's  becoming very it used to be a cheap fuel  it's becoming very expensive now and  they do have to find other places the  netherlands even where we are now they  imported russian coal poland which has  its own mines they had to step up mining  on uh in poland within poland to  increase production there they were  quite reliant and italy so these were  the main import countries and they all  really have to  find new suppliers and they really have  to sort of step up imports in order to  make it through the coming winters  barbara thank you very much our reporter  barbara basil there in rotterdam
 

